5. What comes from others.
   From openai/math: the circuit model in which the result is stated
   (lean/OAI/Computability/FourierCircuit/Core.lean), the theorems on the
   cost of tensor products that the proof uses (the other 43 files of
   section 1), and the one theorem repeated with its proof in
   lean/Work/SmallWHT/TensorPow8.lean (section 2). The 20-gate circuit and
   the other proofs were made for this result.
   Small circuits for this transform were published before by others, among
   them Coppersmith, Feig and Linzer (1994) and Alman and Rao (2023).
   README.md says what is theirs, with references. No file of theirs is
   included here.
   Some of the search programs under tools/circuits/search/ use NumPy,
   and one of them uses SciPy (scipy.optimize.milp, which calls the
   open-source solver HiGHS). tools/circuits/fast/wht56/fast.py, some
   of the tests beside it and paper/check.py use NumPy. Of the programs
   under waves/, 83 use NumPy, SciPy or SymPy, and one C program there
   uses OpenMP. None of these libraries is included here.
   The programs added in revision 2 (measurements/2026-10-22/ and
   tools/fht-kernel/) use, without including them: FFHT (MIT; section 7),
   which their scripts fetch; SIMDe (https://github.com/simd-everywhere/simde,
   MIT), used only to check the x86 code on machines without x86
   processors; the compilers gcc and clang; on the GPU, the NVIDIA driver,
   CUDA and PyTorch, and three libraries that the GPU scripts fetch:
   Dao-AILab/fast-hadamard-transform (BSD 3-Clause), HadaCore in
   pytorch-labs/applied-ai (BSD 3-Clause) and VkFFT (MIT); for the Xilinx
   run, the openXC7 tools and the oss-cad-suite of YosysHQ, which its script
   downloads.

