7. The folders tools/circuits/fpga/ and
   tools/circuits/fast/wht56/results/ffht/.
   The files in these two folders are Jacob Sussman's. They were made on
   2026-10-11 by the AI agents (Anthropic's Claude) that he directed, and
   they are published under the Apache License, Version 2.0, like the rest
   of this repository.
   tools/circuits/fpga/ holds the circuits as hardware: the program that
   writes hardware descriptions, the design files it wrote, the scripts
   that run the open-source hardware tools, the result tables, and the
   sources of a self-test design for a board.
   tools/circuits/fast/wht56/results/ffht/ holds the timings of the 8-point
   step inside the library FFHT: a patch against one file of FFHT, the
   timing programs and what they printed.
   Names of folders and of machines were taken out of the programs and of
   the recorded outputs before publication; the two README files there say
   what else was changed.
   FFHT (https://github.com/FALCONN-LIB/FFHT, at commit
   fba727a3ff72c862260460631ae1cc8e3e44e861) is published under the MIT
   License, copyright (c) 2015 Alexandr Andoni, Piotr Indyk, Thijs
   Laarhoven, Ilya Razenshteyn, Ludwig Schmidt. No file of FFHT is
   included in this repository. The file
   tools/circuits/fast/wht56/results/ffht/style-generator/gen.py.patch is
   a patch against FFHT's gen.py. Besides the new lines it holds the few
   unchanged lines of that file that a patch carries around each change;
   those lines are FFHT's and stay under the MIT License. The files
   plans.json, plans.inc and plans.txt under
   tools/circuits/fast/wht56/results/ffht/style-intrinsics/ are new files
   that describe FFHT's plan of passes (which levels of the transform go
   into which pass at each size), read from the code that FFHT's
   generator writes; the plan is FFHT's. The commands of the README in
   that folder fetch FFHT.
   The hardware descriptions of two video encoders by others, xk265 and
   xk264 (https://github.com/openasic-org/xk265 and
   https://github.com/openasic-org/xk264), were built for comparison and
   are not included: their repositories have no licence file, and the
   headers of their files forbid redistribution. The wrappers under
   tools/circuits/fpga/outside/wrappers/ are new files. They name the
   modules and the ports of those two designs and contain none of their
   code. The commands of tools/circuits/fpga/README.md fetch the two
   repositories.
   The hardware tools (the oss-cad-suite of YosysHQ, with Yosys, nextpnr,
   Icarus Verilog, Verilator and the solvers it ships) and the compiler
   gcc are not included.

8. The folders measurements/2026-10-22/ and tools/fht-kernel/.
   The files in these two folders are Jacob Sussman's. They were made on
   2026-10-21 and 2026-10-22 by the AI agents (Anthropic's Claude) that he
   directed, and they are published under the Apache License, Version 2.0,
   like the rest of this repository, except for the parts of FFHT named
   next.
   Parts of FFHT (section 7 gives its licence and copyright): its recursion
   thresholds (from its hall_of_fame_avx.txt), the instruction sequences of
   its in-lane levels (from its gen.py, written again with intrinsics) and
   its order of passes are FFHT's and stay under the MIT License. They are
   in tools/fht-kernel/whtc_ffht_plan.h, and in bench_hyb.c and
   bench_h512.c of the folders measurements/2026-10-22/x86/harness-hybrid/,
   harness-hybrid512/ and harness-small/. The C++ overloads in
   tools/fht-kernel/whtc.h are copied from FFHT's fht.h and stay under the
   MIT License too. tools/fht-kernel/LICENSE-FFHT reproduces FFHT's licence
   with its copyright line, and a copy of it is in each of the three
   harness folders named above, so that a folder copied on its own keeps
   the licence of the FFHT parts it holds. No file of FFHT is included;
   the scripts fetch it.
   Dao-AILab/fast-hadamard-transform (BSD 3-Clause, read at commit
   e7706faf8d1c3b9f241e36860640ad1dac644ede): the header
   measurements/2026-10-22/gpu/kpart/dao-cse/dao_fast_hadamard_transform_special.h
   defines functions with the same names as Dao's generated header, for
   the same matrices, and is new code, written by the program in
   gpu/kpart/generator/. The files under gpu/kpart/generator/search/mats/
   are the signs of those Hadamard matrices, as Dao's and executorch's code
   generators define them, and nothing else. The GPU scripts patch a
   fetched copy of Dao's library on the test machine; the job that uses
   the header above puts Dao's copyright lines back on top of it there. No
   file of Dao's is included.
   HadaCore (pytorch-labs/applied-ai, BSD 3-Clause; commit 2391954b on the
   day of the run) and VkFFT (MIT, commit 066a17c1) are fetched by the GPU
   script and not included.
   xk265 is still not included: the Artix-7 run left out the folder of
   those designs, and of the designs it placed only those of this
   repository's circuits are published.
   Paths on Jacob Sussman's computer, the names of the machines that
